---
import Article from '../layouts/Article.astro';
import ClearanceDiagram from '../components/ClearanceDiagram.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site';

const toc = [
  { id: 'filters', label: 'Clean the indoor filters' },
  { id: 'outdoor', label: 'Keep the outdoor unit clear' },
  { id: 'temperature', label: 'Don’t set it too low' },
  { id: 'warranty', label: 'Maintenance and your warranty' },
  { id: 'replace', label: 'Replace an old unit' },
];
---

<Article
  title="Air Conditioning Maintenance Tips"
  description="Simple maintenance tips to keep your reverse cycle air conditioner running efficiently: cleaning filters on split and ducted systems, keeping the outdoor unit clear, and more."
  crumb="Maintenance tips"
  heading="Air conditioning maintenance tips."
  lede="A few simple jobs help your reverse cycle system perform at its best, keep your energy bills down and protect your warranty."
  photo="technicianGauges"
  toc={toc}
>
  <div class="callout">
    <Icon name="triangle-alert" />
    <div>
      <p><strong>Safety first.</strong> Always switch your air conditioner off completely before opening any cover, to prevent injury or damage to the unit.</p>
      <p>It’s worth discussing your system’s maintenance needs with your supplier, and reading <a href="/how-to-reduce-air-conditioning-running-costs">how to reduce running costs</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="filters">Clean the filters of the indoor unit</h2>
  <p>
    Dirty filters reduce performance because they make the unit work harder, and it can overheat. Research from Canstar Blue
    found 41% of respondents had never cleaned the filters on their air conditioner. Dust builds up, restricts the airflow and
    pushes up your energy bills.
  </p>
  <h3>Wall-mounted split systems</h3>
  <p>
    Lift the front cover and remove the filters, noting how they’re arranged, as some models have extra deodorising or
    air-purification filters. Clean them the way your owner’s manual describes. Some filters can be rinsed under a tap and
    some can’t, so check first, and if you use water, let them dry completely before you put them back.
  </p>
  <h3>Ducted systems</h3>
  <p>
    Find the return air grille, usually in a central spot such as the hallway. Undo the screws holding the grille and carefully
    remove the filter pad. Clean it the way your manual recommends, and if you use water, make sure it’s completely dry before
    you put it back.
  </p>

  <h2 id="outdoor">Keep the outdoor unit free from debris</h2>
  <p>
    A build-up of leaves or rubbish on the outdoor unit restricts airflow, so the compressor works harder and your power bills
    go up. Don’t let plants such as creepers grow into the unit, as they can damage it.
  </p>
  <ClearanceDiagram id="maint-clear" />
  <p>Keep at least <strong>10 cm clear all around</strong> the unit, and around <strong>100 cm clear in front of the fan</strong>.</p>

  <h2 id="temperature">Don’t set the temperature too low</h2>
  <p>
    On hot days, setting the coldest temperature won’t cool your home any faster, and it puts extra pressure on your system.
    For Fujitsu reverse cycle systems, setting the temperature above 23°C is recommended to minimise energy use.
  </p>

  <h2 id="warranty">Maintenance and your warranty</h2>
  <p>
    Warranties only cover manufacturing and design defects or faulty workmanship. If a warranty service call turns out to be
    caused by poor maintenance, you may be charged for it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="replace">Save money by replacing an old unit</h2>
  <p>
    An old unit can mean much higher energy bills and costly repairs. Replacing your existing ducted system with a new
    high-efficiency inverter will reduce your bills and save you money every year. See our
    <a href="/air-conditioning-adelaide/ducted-air-conditioning-replacement-units">ducted replacement units</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    For more advice on maintaining your reverse cycle system, call us on <a href={site.phone.href}>{site.phone.display}</a>.
  </p>
</Article>
